program            = 007_alignof_alignas
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = _Alignof and _Alignas asserted by exact equality, with alignment observed only as residues and never through addresses
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every object read is written first. local8[0], p.c and p.aligned_member are all assigned
before the readback line prints them, and buf16 and wide_aligned have static storage duration
so they are initialized before main begins. Only element 0 of local8 is accessed, well inside
its eight elements, and only element 0 of buf16 is ever addressed, never dereferenced beyond
its bounds. The four address values are obtained by converting an object pointer first to
unsigned long, which is exactly pointer width on every target, and only then widening that
result to unsigned long long, which is eight bytes everywhere; no conversion crosses a width
boundary, so no pointer value is ever truncated. Those converted values are used only as the
left operand of a remainder against a positive ULL constant, which is well defined, and the
results are small so the casts back to int are value preserving. Each residue depends only on
that object's own address modulo that object's own required alignment; no residue depends on
the relationship between two objects, so there is no dependence on the relative addresses of
unrelated objects. No address value ever reaches stdout. All other arithmetic is on small int
values far from the range edges, so there is no signed overflow. No shift is performed. No
object is modified twice between sequence points and no printf call receives a side-effecting
argument. There is no aliasing violation: every object is accessed only through an lvalue of
its own declared type, and the unsigned char arrays are never reinterpreted as another type.
No padding byte is read -- p.c and p.aligned_member are named members, and the padding the
_Alignas(8) member introduces between them is never accessed.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
RESIDUE TECHNIQUE. An address is never reproducible: it varies with the loader, the target, the
optimization level and whether execution is native or under emulation, so printing one would
make byte-exact comparison meaningless and would generate divergences in every cell that say
nothing about compiler correctness. This program therefore prints only alignment residues. Each
object's address is carried through a two-step conversion: the pointer is cast first to
unsigned long, which is exactly pointer width on all four targets, and that result is then
widened to unsigned long long, which is eight bytes everywhere and so always holds the residue
arithmetic. uintptr_t is unavailable because this program includes no header -- the corpus's
only sanctioned inclusion is area 07's <stdarg.h>, and stdint.h, though bundled, is not
sanctioned here -- which is why unsigned long is the carrier. The two-step form is deliberate
rather than incidental: casting a pointer directly to unsigned long long crosses a width
boundary on i686, where it is diagnosed as -Wpointer-to-int-cast and escalated to an error by
the mandatory audit gate, so the single-step spelling would fail that gate on one of the four
targets this record declares. Only the value modulo that object's own requested alignment is
printed. Every residue must be 0 on every target at every optimization level, which is exactly
what _Alignas promises. The address itself never reaches stdout.

MEASURED I686 ALIGNMENT DIFFERENCE, HANDLED BY AN EXACT PER-TARGET EXPECTATION RATHER THAN BY
RESTRICTION. On the i386 System V ABI, _Alignof(double), _Alignof(long long), _Alignof(long) and
_Alignof(void *) are all 4, whereas on x86_64, aarch64 and riscv64 they are 8, and sizeof(void *)
and sizeof(long) vary with them. Printing any of those six raw would be a legitimate
implementation-defined difference that oracle (b) would misreport as a defect. Raw alignments are
therefore printed only for char (1), short (2), int (4) and float (4), which were measured
identical on all four targets. The six width-varying quantities are still exercised -- no feature
is dropped for being awkward -- but each is asserted as an EQUALITY against the one number the
target must use, selected by the architecture macro the compiler predefines. EXPECTED_WIDE_ALIGN
is 8 for __x86_64__, 4 for __i386__, 8 for __aarch64__ and 8 for __riscv, so every printed
predicate is 1 on every target and stdout stays byte-identical. The unrecognised-architecture
branch is an #error rather than a silent default, so a fifth target cannot acquire an unchecked
expectation. Because the difference is neutralized inside the program, no target restriction is
applied and all four targets run.

A PERMISSIVE RANGE IS DELIBERATELY NOT USED. A bounded relation such as "at least 4 and at most 8"
would also print 1 on all four targets, but it accepts an alignment of 5, 6 or 7 and it accepts
the wrong choice between 4 and 8, so a real code-generation defect would still print 1 and
oracle (a), oracle (b) and the golden record would all agree with it. Exact equality is what makes
the line discriminating. The expectation is keyed to the ARCHITECTURE macro rather than to
__SIZEOF_POINTER__ or any other width macro, because the architecture is a categorical fact the
harness fixes with --target, whereas a width macro is a numeric claim by the compiler under test,
and asserting that claim against itself would be circular.

SIZES PINNED ALONGSIDE ALIGNMENTS. sizeof(void *) and sizeof(long) are pinned to the same
architecture-selected number, and sizeof(double) and sizeof(long long) to 8 outright, because a
compiler that got both an alignment and the corresponding width wrong in the same direction could
otherwise still satisfy the alignment line on its own.

ALIGNAS PLACEMENT. _Alignas is an alignment specifier legal only in the declaration specifiers
of an object or a struct member; attaching it to a struct tag as struct _Alignas(16) Tag is a
syntax error. The struct therefore carries _Alignas(8) on its int member, which raises the
whole struct's alignment to 8 and its size to 16 as a standard-mandated consequence: the char
member occupies offset 0, the aligned member is forced to offset 8, and the 12-byte extent is
rounded up to the struct's 8-byte alignment. Those two values are therefore target-stable and
are printed raw.

KEYWORD SPELLINGS. The lowercase alignas and alignof macros live in the bundled header
stdalign.h, which this program does not include, so the C11 keywords _Alignas and _Alignof are
used directly. Both are documented as supported. No plain char signedness dependence exists:
the buffers are unsigned char, and the one plain char member holds the small positive value 2,
which is representable identically whether char is signed or unsigned. The only printf
conversion specifier used in the program is %d.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
alignof_stable=1 2 4 4
alignof_exact=1 1 1 1
sizeof_exact=1 1 1 1
alignas_struct=8 16
residues=0 0 0 0
readback=1 2 3
END
